            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-3",
                    "linecount": 27,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 420.0, 410.0 ],
                    "text": "ClipOnly3 — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/cliponly3/\n\nWhat it is: a transparent \"safety brickwall\". It only acts on audio that\nwould actually clip: below ±0.90850431 it changes nothing at all, so it is\nsafe to leave on any channel or buss. This RNBO port has NO parameters - the\nthreshold, soft-clip shape and slew control are fixed in the algorithm.\n\nNo parameters:\n\n- (none)\n  Internal behavior, for reference:\n  * soft-clip threshold ±0.9085097 (about -0.84 dBFS), hard overshoot\n    reconstruction to keep clipped tops from sounding \"digital\"\n  * fixes clipped tops in a very narrow band, not the whole waveform\n  * behaves like a nonlinear lowpass control (based on slew) to tame\n    overs and digital harshness without dulling the material\n  * consumes a small amount of CPU; designed never to be bypassed, you\n    just leave it in the chain\n\nMusical notes: use it where you would otherwise reach for a limiter but\ndon't want one - before a saturator or clipper, or on a buss you keep\nrelatively unprocessed. It prints a slightly warmer \"slide\" over any\novershoots rather than a hard-topped square.\n\nSource quirks (see RNBO/README.md): the port initializes lastDryL/R to 0\n(the source leaves them undefined); behavior otherwise matches the source.\n",
                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                }
            }
        ],
